      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To study the degree of pain caused by double-contrast barium enema examination and colonoscopy and to compare pain ratings by patients and medical personnel."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "In a random sample, 190 patients (aged 45-79 years) (mean, 63.1 years) recorded their experiences of pain during each procedure and the medical staff recorded their impressions of the patients' pain, on a 10-point progressive scale. The data were analyzed with the Wilcoxon rank test, Spearman rank correlation, and generalized kappa value."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Patients' pain ratings were significantly higher during colonoscopy than during double-contrast barium enema examination (P < .0001). Pain was rated significantly higher by patients than by personnel for both procedures (P < .0001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In the choice of procedure, patients' high level of pain during colonoscopy should be remembered. The medical staff frequently underestimated patients' pain during both procedures, and this study documents a need for more extensive pain relief measures during potentially painful examinations of the large bowel."
        }
      ]},
